id: rust.generics-monomorphization
kind: rust
title: Generics and monomorphization
aliases: monomorphization, generic code size
tags: fundamentals, performance
questions:
  - what is the runtime cost of generics in Rust?
invariant: Generic code is compiled once per concrete type, so dispatch is free at runtime and the cost is binary size plus compile time.
answer_30s: Generics are monomorphised: you pay at compile time and in binary size, and get zero-cost static dispatch at runtime.
when_to_use:
  - library internals and hot paths where dispatch overhead would be measurable
avoid_when:
  - huge generic layers where a dyn boundary or enum dispatch is clearer and smaller
failure_modes:
  - a public API that instantiates a large generic stack for many types, bloating builds
  - a deeply generic async stack producing very large futures (the future-too-large warning, fixed by boxing)
related:
  - rust.traits
  - rust.dyn-vs-generic
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-01-syntax.html
